; expect 38
; `type` and `effect` templates (MAC-CAP-8's last two kinds,
; 2026-08-15). A declaration template could generate `fn`, `::`,
; `data`, `struct`, `impl` and further invocations; the two remaining
; families were `AX3021` at the macro's own line. What is left
; refusing is refused by DECISION, not schedule: `import` reopens
; resolution ordering and a nested `macro` would join the table the
; phase-D fixpoint is reading (tests/diagnostics/565).
;
; Both kinds needed the same thing the type kinds needed and one more:
; their NAMES go through `parseDeclName`, so a template can join them
; - and a joined name has to be writable in TYPE position too, since
; the signature beside a generated alias is the first thing that wants
; to name it. `(syntax/join nm Handle)` had no type-position parse: a
; type application needs an UPPERCASE head, so the form fell into the
; tuple branch and a generated signature took a three-tuple where its
; alias belonged, reported at the CALL SITE as `expected Int, found
; (_t321, {unknown}, {unknown})`.
;
;   8   the alias exists, is named `BoxHandle` by a join, and the
;       generated signature beside it names that alias in type
;       position - so the wrapper type-checks and answers its argument
;  30   the effect exists, is named by the invocation's argument, its
;       operation is named by another, its declared arrow came through
;       type substitution, and a handler installed here runs it
;
; The unfixed compiler refuses this file at the `type` line with
; `AX2003 syntax error`, exit 1 - the joined alias name did not parse
; there, which is one layer earlier than the `AX3021` an unsupported
; template kind draws.
(import IO)

;; a `type` template, and a `::`/`fn` pair that names what it declared
(pub macro mkAlias ((mkAlias nm T)
   (type (syntax/join nm Handle) = T)
   (:: (syntax/join wrap nm) (-> T (syntax/join nm Handle)))
   (fn ((syntax/join wrap nm) v) v)))

(mkAlias Box Int)

;; an `effect` template: the effect's name, its operation's name and
;; its operation's arrow are all the invocation's
(pub macro mkEffect ((mkEffect E op T)
   (effect E
     (op :: (-> T T)))))

(mkEffect Tick bump Int)

;@axiom:effect(Tick)
(:: useIt (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(Tick)
(fn (useIt n) (bump n))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (+
    (wrapBox 8)    (handle (useIt 3) (Tick) (lambda (v) (+ v 27)))
  )
)
